00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.490
In Secret Manager, a secret
is a logical collection

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.140
of secret versions.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.380
Versioning is a core
tenant of reliable systems

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.560
to support gradual rollout,
emergency rollback,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.940
and auditing.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.090
Secret Manager automatically
versions secret data

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.300
using secret versions,
and most operations

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.840
like access, destroy,
disable, and enable all

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.330
take place on a secret version.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.060
While the parent secret
holds metadata information,

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.640
like name and
replication policy,

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.730
the secret version stores
the actual secret payload,

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.830
your secret data.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.840
Once a secret
version is created,

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.610
its payload is immutable.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.769
That means version
42 of a secret

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:07.620
will always contain
the same payload.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.630
When a new secret
version is added,

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:13.260
it automatically becomes
the latest version.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.710
So should your applications
pin to a specific version,

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.750
or should they just use latest?

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.585
To answer that question, I think
we need to bring in an expert.

00:01:21.585 --> 00:01:24.770
[FUNK MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:25.980
Hey, Rachana.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.490
Thanks for joining us today.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:28.782
RACHANA LINGUTLA: Thanks, Seth.

00:01:28.782 --> 00:01:30.400
Thank you for having me.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.970
SETH VARGO: So Rachana is
a software engineer who

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.550
works on Google Secret Manager.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:36.480
OK, Rachana.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.430
Earlier, I was
telling our viewers

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:42.630
about secret versioning
and the latest alias.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.520
When should we pin
our applications

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.352
to a specific version, and when
would we use this latest alias?

00:01:48.352 --> 00:01:49.560
RACHANA LINGUTLA: Sure, Seth.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.300
To help answer that
question, let's look

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.430
at a hypothetical scenario.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.230
Suppose we have an
application that

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.890
uses an API key to communicate
with a third-party service.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:02.010
If the application was
using the latest alias,

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:05.580
Secret Manager would always
return the most recent value,

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.120
regardless of whether that
value was ready to be consumed.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.240
If the application was
pinned to a specific version,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.670
Secret Manager would
return the pinned version,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.340
even if a newer version exists.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.210
SETH VARGO: OK, but then
if I pay my application

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.180
to a specific
secret version, how

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.210
would I update the secret
used by that application?

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.580
Do I have to package and
rebundle and redeploy

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:30.827
my whole application?

00:02:30.827 --> 00:02:32.160
RACHANA LINGUTLA: Actually, yes.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.770
At Google, we believe
secrets are configuration,

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.510
and configuration
should be versioned

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.950
with the deployable artifact.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.270
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:42.340
That kind of makes sense.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.350
So that way if there's
a problem or a bug,

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:49.460
I can easily rollback,
and if I rollback,

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.410
my configuration would include
the old secret version.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.220
So my application
is then bundled

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.110
in a known working
version, right?

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.245
RACHANA LINGUTLA: Exactly.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:02:59.870
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.560
OK, so it seems like
the latest alias is not

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:04.520
good for production.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.230
But it'd be really
useful when I'm

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:09.650
doing rapid iteration in
my development or staging

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:10.310
environments.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:11.300
Is that right?

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.547
RACHANA LINGUTLA: Yes,
that's right, Seth.

00:03:13.547 --> 00:03:14.380
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.590
Thanks, Rachana.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:17.250
OK.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:18.990
Let's look at a
Google Cloud function

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:23.220
that accesses a secret using
the Secret Manager API.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.370
While iterating in my local
development environment,

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.070
I'd instruct my Cloud Function
to access the secret using

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.870
the latest alias.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.480
But when I'm ready to
move to production,

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.740
I would pin my deployment to
a specific secret version.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.260
Secret Manager's
first-class versioning

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.570
helps you follow a
DevOps best practice

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:46.410
of reproducible deployments,
while the latest alias provides

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.130
a convenient method for rapidly
iterating during development.

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:51.840
That's all for this episode.

